suterb42@gmail.com wrote:

> I'm using the newest version of MinGW to compile NH, and everything works fine until it comes to running makedefs -v. When it gets there, makedefs stops working. This happens every time I try to compile. Every other makedefs switch works. Does anyone know what's going on?

Given the "Windows" and "64-bit" hints, I strongly suspect that the bug
is on line 566 of util/makedefs.c. Comparing the (buggy) vanilla 3.4.3
code to the code in NetHack 4 (which has this bug fixed), we can see
that it's attempting to create a time_t pointer to a long int variable.
Although incorrect, this isn't a problem on most OS/processor
combinations; however, long ints are always 32 bits on Windows, whereas
I strongly suspect that on 64-bit Windows, a time_t is 64 bits wide. So
the devteam's code is trashing four bytes of memory on the stack
somewhere.

Probably the simplest fix (apart from using a codebase where these
problems have already been fixed) is to change clocktim to be a time_t
on line 546 of util/makedefs.c, and add a (long) cast to the use of
clocktim on line 572; this approximates the fixes for this problem used
in the variants.

Incidentally, this bug doesn't seem to be on the official NetHack 3.4.3
buglist. It may be that the devteam never attempted to produce a 64-bit
Windows version of NetHack (because the 32-bit version runs fine on
64-bit Windows). However, I feel it would be quite disappointing if
after years of cross-platform support, they neglected such a major
platform as this.

